    Overlap-Aware & $\checkmark$ & 2 & 2 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 12 \\
    Similarity-Based & $\checkmark$ & 1 & 2 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\
    GA/PSO/ABC & $\checkmark$ & 1 & 3 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 8 \\
    REDUNET & $\times$ & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & 0 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Selection Decision}

\textbf{MA-EHGS is selected for LASSO integration}, achieving the highest total score of 23/25, outperforming Classical Greedy (22/25) due to explicit mutation-awareness in criterion C4.

\subsection{Mutation-Awareness Advantage}

Coverage-only minimisation loses 0--5\% mutation capability~\cite{jehan2023}. Wei et al.~\cite{wei2017} demonstrate that integrating mutation score as an optimisation objective achieves 95--100\% retention. MA-EHGS implements this via $\beta$-parameter weighting, achieving C4 score of 5/5 versus Classical Greedy's 3/5. This directly addresses the documented gap.

\subsection{Multi-Criteria Optimality}

MA-EHGS excels across all dimensions: 100\% coverage guarantee (C2), strong reduction (C3: 4/5), optimal scalability (C5), and straightforward implementation (C6). Alternatives trade critical properties: Delayed Greedy has perfect mutation preservation (5/5) but prohibitive $O(n^2m)$ complexity; Many-Objective EA offers strong mutation preservation (5/5) but requires extensive parameter tuning and incurs higher computational cost.

\subsection{Practical Advantages for LASSO}

MA-EHGS operates on requirements matrices, aligning with LASSO's SRM abstraction without CFG dependencies. Polynomial $O(nm)$ complexity enables processing large SRMs (1,000+ tests, 500+ requirements) in seconds. The $\beta$-parameter permits empirical tuning via grid search ($\beta \in \{0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9\}$), with $\beta=0$ reproducing the Enhanced HGS baseline~\cite{gladston2015} and $\beta=0.7$ balancing coverage and mutation emphasis.

\section{Conclusion}

MA-EHGS provides a scalable, mutation-aware test-suite minimisation approach suitable for LASSO's language-independent, large-scale analysis. Selection is validated by analysis of 33 reference papers, with particular support from Wei et al.~\cite{wei2017} demonstrating that mutation-aware optimisation effectively addresses the coverage-mutation trade-off documented in recent literature~\cite{jehan2023}.